plan Cutover
title   Cutover
status  archived
goal
    Reach the accepted replacement boundary only after mapped proofs and cutover
    gates establish the replacement and remove deleted behavior. Workflow Plans own
    migrated semantics; source contracts provide provenance and trace IDs. This Plan
    owns disposition, source cleanup, and product cutover evidence. No Plan claim means
    implementation, physical source cleanup, or product cutover is complete.

outcomes
    O1  One accepted package boundary
        Cutover exposes one accepted surface across installed packages, product
        documentation, runtimes and generated or handwritten declarations. Retained
        environmental boundaries remain available; deleted surfaces are absent.
    O2  Complete disposition
        Classify every affected old clause, symbol, overload, registry, consumer,
        proof and task as DELETE, REPLACE, RETAIN or HISTORICAL. Record its accepted
        replacement or retained boundary.
    O3  Evidence-backed implementation and product cutover
        Semantic mapping is complete; Workflow semantic authority and source status
        transfer now. Remaining traceability and completeness checks, physical removal,
        and product cutover wait for proof and no-residue gates.
        Package promotion and product cutover remain pending; no Plan asserts
        implementation is complete.

decisions
    D1  Workflow semantic authority and product status
        Workflow Plans own migrated semantics and proof routing. Source contracts
        provide trace IDs and migration evidence; they do not override Plan semantics.
        This transfers rewrite requirements only. Product documentation keeps its
        owner; Workflow Tasks own current task status. Beads records are historical
        snapshots, not current readiness or authority. Implementation, physical source
        cleanup, promotion, and product cutover remain pending
        @plan.Verification.V7. A failed proof may
        reopen only its disproved guarantee, never authorize an unaccepted API,
        default, exception, alias, or proof requirement.

        Accepted public decisions stay closed while gaps are resolved. Plans, public
        types, artifacts and proofs move as one replacement. Production-owner proofs
        are required under @plan.Verification.V7. This Plan owns the exhaustive
        old-surface disposition. Its source copy is provenance only. The historical
        behavior register is
        closed by accepted revisions, but migration is not mechanically complete
        until implementation proofs pass.

        Verification owns cross-cutting gates and central proof coverage:
        @plan.Verification.V7. This Plan owns deletion/retention mapping and
        cutover-specific evidence. Story semantics stay at
        @plan.Verification.V4; this reference does not duplicate them.
    D2  Hard deletion and retained boundaries
        Dispositions mean:
        - **DELETE:** no replacement; remove the old surface completely.
        - **REPLACE:** remove the old surface; continue its capability under the
          accepted replacement.
        - **RETAIN:** preserve the mapped boundary under its owning Workflow Plan.
        - **HISTORICAL:** allow the material only in Git history or an explicitly
          historical location, never as active authority.

        REPLACE is hard deletion. It permits no alias, overload, adapter,
        compatibility namespace, parser branch or deprecated wrapper. Environmental
        compatibility exists only in explicit RETAIN rows. An unmapped source clause
        is a migration gap, not an active semantic owner. Cutover waits until every affected
        clause, symbol, overload, registry, consumer, proof and task is classified or
        recorded unchanged.

        Deleted names may appear only in this ledger, the non-normative deferred
        record, historical provenance or a negative absence proof. Never make them
        supported API, replacement guidance, active glossary definitions or
        positive consumer usages. Keep RETAIN rows unchanged unless an accepted
        revision changes their disposition. Reference consumers rewrite deleted
        calls. Selector files may be redistributed; unused host fixtures may be
        removed or promoted into tested hosts. Folder layout is not a public deletion
        rule.
    D3  DEFERRED is non-normative context only
        DEFERRED.md records complexity deliberately removed or relaxed by an
        authorized feature-preserving simplification. Its entries
        are not implementation tasks, Plan outcomes or proof obligations. They may
        return only after the listed trigger exists and a separate contract change is
        accepted. The file promises no future work. The record says routes and
        families in proposed CONTRACT remain in scope; that does not accept its
        proposal clauses or signatures. DEFERRED does not override mapped Workflow
        Plan semantics.

        These twelve entries provide nonbinding context, not implementation duties:
        **Security and carrier boundaries**
        - **Trusted gateway import policing:** Removed computed dynamic-import and
          nonliteral-require scans, dependency graph traversal and ancestor
          node_modules policing. Previous owner: @plan.CLI.V1. The target retains root
          containment, regular .ts/.mts files, trusted unsandboxed execution,
          Flow/Effect identity, branded gateway/app/story validation, inert artifacts
          and temporary-output cleanup. Revisit only for an explicit sandbox,
          hermetic build or untrusted-code security claim.

        - **Hostile JavaScript carrier reflection:** Removed promises for proxies,
          throwing getters, descriptors/accessors, symbols and sparse arrays. Also
          removed promises for custom prototypes, reserved-prototype behavior and
          concurrent mutation passed directly to private decoders. Previous owner:
          @plan.Artifacts.O4. The target
          retains strict UTF-8/file input, one compression member, decompressed bounds
          and JSON. It uses one private Effect Schema decoder to fresh canonical data
          and retains pre-mutation stable failure. Revisit only if a public API accepts
          arbitrary live JS graphs as untrusted carriers.

        **Host portability and runtime ownership**
        - **Full codec vectors on every host:** Removed repeating every Unicode,
          surrogate and limit vector under every host. Previous owners:
          @plan.Verification.D5 and @plan.Verification.V6. The target retains full
          pinned-Node vectors. It also retains a compact same-codec fixture and exact
          version receipts on Bun, Deno, Chromium, Gecko and WebKit. Revisit for a
          host-specific codec/text implementation or an undetected portability failure.

        - **Prescribed Runtime internals:** Removed exact private phases, one
          ManagedRuntime, shell cells, fixed Queue/SubscriptionRef/Deferred topology,
          exact runSync count and source-shape proofs. Previous owner:
          @plan.Runtime.O3. The target retains inert sync construction and at-most-once
          readiness with cached terminal Exit. It releases no handle before readiness.
          It has one lifetime owner, reverse rollback and cleanup on failure,
          interruption or disposal. Revisit only
          if valid implementations differ observably in ownership/readiness/cleanup.

        **Observable ordering and proof ownership**
        - **Unobservable commit micro-order:** Removed ordering of private permit,
          store, issue, snapshot, evidence acceptance, reconciliation enqueue,
          acknowledgement, release gate and StoreFanout steps. Previous owners:
          @plan.Runtime.O3, @plan.Operations.O1 and @plan.Artifacts.O3. The target
          retains one atomic actor/store cut, FIFO/causal ordering and committed
          evidence. Acknowledgement follows publication and evidence acceptance, but
          precedes user Effect settlement. Sinks remain isolated and nonblocking.
          StoreFanout remains causal, and disposal drains. Revisit only if a
          new public receipt/transaction/observation exposes an intermediate phase.

        - **Duplicate semantic proof ownership:** Removed repeating full
          positive/negative/race/boundary matrices across focused tests, validators,
          packed/browser/CLI runs and final verification. Previous owners:
          @plan.Verification.D2, @plan.Verification.D6 and implementation tasks.
          The target retains one named
          production-owner proof per invariant; integrations add reachability and
          distinct failure cases. Revisit only when the owner proof cannot exercise a
          materially different public or host boundary.

        **Performance and capacity limits**
        - **Exact TypeScript benchmark arithmetic:** Removed mandatory
          25-machine/100-descriptor fixture, ten-percent instantiation window,
          doubled-root ratio and numeric growth gates. Previous owner:
          @plan.Verification.V1. The target retains representative small/medium fixtures, exact
          inference, negative
          assertions and declaration correctness on pinned compiler. Compiler
          measurements, repeated samples, baseline freshness and numeric budgets are
          deferred and do not block Phase 1.5. Revisit after the library is built and
          performance work is authorized.

        - **Exact internal capacity cutoffs:** Removed compatibility promise for
          precise failure points of private prepared-command, retention and batch
          capacities. Previous owners: React prepared mailbox and internal vectors.
          The target retains bounds, documented ordinary capacity and failure without
          partial mutation. Caller bounds/defaults and external byte, decompression,
          node and depth limits remain exact. Revisit if clients must size or negotiate an
          internal buffer or a wire/public API exposes its exact capacity.

        **Output and evidence compatibility**
        - **Human-prose compatibility:** Removed byte-stable human errors, CLI
          prose/whitespace/field ordering and snapshots asserting only exact wording.
          Previous owners: @plan.Diagnostics.O1 and @plan.CLI.V3. The target retains
          stable tag/code/path/details, canonical CLI JSON, exit/stdout/stderr behavior
          and one-document writes. It retains actionable nonempty text, a newline and
          no ANSI for noninteractive
          output. Revisit only if Flow publishes human text as a shell-parsing format;
          canonical JSON remains the machine interface.

        - **Dual truncation markers:** Removed separate greatest-dropped and
          first-omitted semantics, conversion arithmetic and exact drain/CAS algorithm.
          Previous owner: @plan.Verification.V6 and v2 trace artifacts. The target retains one
          truncatedBeforeSequence marker: null iff nothing omitted, otherwise greatest
          omitted runtime-global sequence; trace proof rejects non-null. Snapshot,
          clear, attach, detach, drain and sink failure remain atomic/isolated. Revisit
          only for a shipped artifact requiring old interpretation, with explicit
          version boundary.

        - **Serialized Effect Cause traversal:** Removed stable wire coupling to
          Cause tree shape/order/fiber ordinals/multiplicity. Previous owners:
          @plan.Artifacts.O6, @plan.Runtime.O3 and @plan.Verification.V6. The target
          retains full in-process Cause through classification and cleanup. Documented
          public errors keep it. Serialized evidence carries ordered stable Flow
          diagnostics and independently actionable cleanup diagnostic order and
          multiplicity. Revisit only for a
          supported consumer needing lossless Effect-internal reconstruction and
          accepting explicit Effect-version coupling.

        **Historical inventory scope**
        - **Repeated historical absence inventories:** Removed full deletion
          inventory/name matrix from every implementation/validation Bead. Previous
          owners: @O2, @plan.Verification.V7 and cutover tasks. The target retains the
          authoritative ledger, export/declaration negatives, one mapped
          final no-consumer audit and feature-owner proof only for intersecting
          behavior. Revisit only if a removed surface reappears or compatibility is
          proposed.
    D4  Historical behavior is provenance, not a compatibility source
        The historical behavior register is provenance, not compatibility input.
        Accepted operation and host changes close the recorded runtime cases.
        Artifact and diagnostic changes close the remaining recorded migration cases.
        Workflow Tasks carry named implementation obligations; Beads records and old
        proof receipts are historical evidence. No inherited behavior entry blocks
        compatibility. History does not select behavior.

constraints
    C1  Target-only status
        Workflow Plans define target semantics, not shipped code/API/behavior.
        Neither the Plans nor their source trace IDs prove implementation or promotion.
        Product cutover stays pending until all mapped implementation and proof gates pass.
    C2  No coexistence or compatibility translation
        No installed package, current product documentation, runtime or generated /
        handwritten type may expose or compose deleted and replacement surfaces together.
        Replacement is hard deletion. Removed values, types, overloads, properties,
        deep imports, registries, consumers, proofs and task references fail closed.
        Rejection occurs before actor creation, memory mutation, operation
        acquisition, event publication, external work or evidence.
    C3  Semantic transfer before source cleanup
        Workflow Plans own migrated semantics; source status banners identify their
        files as provenance. Keep them for traceability until mapped proofs and the
        no-residue audit authorize physical removal. Product documentation keeps its
        owner; Workflow Tasks own current status and Beads records remain historical
        snapshots. This does not claim product cutover.
    C4  Shared proof owner
        Cross-cutting final gates are owned by @plan.Verification.V7. This Plan owns
        cutover scenarios, disposition detail and repository no-residue
        interpretation; do not copy
        the complete proof matrix here.
    C5  Source traceability
        - Migration sources and trace IDs are
          reference/incident-console/implementation/contracts/COMPATIBILITY_AND_DELETIONS.md
          and reference/incident-console/implementation/contracts/PROOF_MATRIX.md.
        - Context only: reference/incident-console/implementation/contracts/DEFERRED.md.
          It is nonnormative and nonbinding; it creates no implementation or proof
          obligation.
        - Proposal only: reference/incident-console/implementation/contracts/CONTRACT.md
          §§8, 11 and 12. They remain unaccepted examples, checklist and provenance.
        - Workflow Plans own migrated semantic requirements. Source clauses provide
          traceability only; status banners now record provenance. Physical source
          cleanup remains gated by mapped proofs and the no-residue audit.

    C6  Source-to-Plan proof crosswalk
        Source IDs below preserve traceability; Workflow Plan outcomes own the
        migrated semantics. Central and local proof mappings follow PROOF_MATRIX.md.

